1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,181
This QR code doesn't point to
a program stored somewhere.

2
00:00:03,181 --> 00:00:04,528
It is the program.

3
00:00:04,528 --> 00:00:06,920
Scan it, and the address
it holds contains,

4
00:00:06,920 --> 00:00:08,789
spelled out in a compact alphabet,

5
00:00:08,789 --> 00:00:10,565
a tiny Graphviz diagram:

6
00:00:10,565 --> 00:00:13,096
scan, load, edit, render, share,

7
00:00:13,096 --> 00:00:15,871
and back to scan. No server
needed to look it up,

8
00:00:15,871 --> 00:00:17,647
because there's nothing to look up.

9
00:00:17,647 --> 00:00:21,049
Most programs are far too big to
fit inside an address like that.

10
00:00:21,049 --> 00:00:24,404
But this little one shows the
destination of this final episode:

11
00:00:24,404 --> 00:00:27,492
how a static website, with
no database of its own,

12
00:00:27,492 --> 00:00:30,348
lets you share code
through links and QR codes,

13
00:00:30,348 --> 00:00:33,533
and why those links can be trusted.

14
00:00:33,533 --> 00:00:36,003
When you click Share,
where does your code go?

15
00:00:36,003 --> 00:00:39,698
ManimOnline has no server
of its own and no database.

16
00:00:39,698 --> 00:00:42,600
It's just files. So it has two options.

17
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:45,631
Option one: put the code
inside the link itself.

18
00:00:45,631 --> 00:00:47,906
Option two: store the code somewhere,

19
00:00:47,906 --> 00:00:49,776
and put a name for it in the link.

20
00:00:49,776 --> 00:00:52,341
Both are used, and each
solves a different problem.

21
00:00:52,341 --> 00:00:55,639
And option two hides a
really interesting question:

22
00:00:55,639 --> 00:00:57,917
who gets to choose the name?

23
00:00:57,917 --> 00:00:59,508
The first option sounds easy.

24
00:00:59,508 --> 00:01:02,770
Take the source code, and put it
in the address after a hash sign,

25
00:01:02,770 --> 00:01:05,487
the part of a link that
browsers never send to a server.

26
00:01:05,487 --> 00:01:07,066
But addresses have rules:

27
00:01:07,066 --> 00:01:11,222
spaces, new lines, colons and equals
signs aren't allowed as they are,

28
00:01:11,222 --> 00:01:14,392
so each one gets replaced
by a three-character code.

29
00:01:14,392 --> 00:01:17,747
Our little scene grows from two hundred
and forty nine characters to three

30
00:01:17,747 --> 00:01:19,245
hundred and ninety one.

31
00:01:19,245 --> 00:01:20,939
Real scenes get long fast.

32
00:01:20,939 --> 00:01:22,112
Look at the code again, though.

33
00:01:22,112 --> 00:01:23,169
It repeats itself.

34
00:01:23,169 --> 00:01:24,585
Self dot play, twice.

35
00:01:24,585 --> 00:01:26,292
Indentation, over and over.

36
00:01:26,292 --> 00:01:27,592
Colour equals, twice.

37
00:01:27,592 --> 00:01:32,700
Code is full of repetition, and repetition
is exactly what compression feeds on.

38
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:36,427
Here's the core idea of a whole
family of compression methods,

39
00:01:36,427 --> 00:01:41,500
first described by Abraham Lempel and
Jacob Ziv in the late nineteen seventies.

40
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:43,579
Read the text from left to right.

41
00:01:43,579 --> 00:01:46,295
Whenever the next few
characters have appeared before,

42
00:01:46,295 --> 00:01:47,630
don't write them out again.

43
00:01:47,630 --> 00:01:49,256
Write a note instead:

44
00:01:49,256 --> 00:01:52,437
go back this many characters,
and copy this many.

45
00:01:52,437 --> 00:01:55,862
At first everything is new, so
letters are copied as they are.

46
00:01:55,862 --> 00:02:00,099
But "at space", in "sat",
already appeared in "cat",

47
00:02:00,099 --> 00:02:01,319
four characters back.

48
00:02:01,319 --> 00:02:03,931
So we write: back four, copy three.

49
00:02:03,931 --> 00:02:07,425
Later, "the" reappears,
fifteen characters back.

50
00:02:07,425 --> 00:02:11,396
And "cat sat" at the end can be copied
in one go from twenty four characters

51
00:02:11,396 --> 00:02:11,884
back.

52
00:02:11,884 --> 00:02:15,587
Thirty five characters became
sixteen letters and four references.

53
00:02:15,587 --> 00:02:18,153
On a short sentence
that's a modest saving.

54
00:02:18,153 --> 00:02:20,336
On source code, where whole lines repeat,

55
00:02:20,336 --> 00:02:23,564
it's dramatic, and decompressing
is just following the notes.

56
00:02:23,564 --> 00:02:28,195
ManimOnline's Share button uses
a library called lz-string,

57
00:02:28,195 --> 00:02:29,356
from the same family.

58
00:02:29,356 --> 00:02:32,259
It builds a dictionary of
phrases it has already seen,

59
00:02:32,259 --> 00:02:37,117
and writes its output in characters that
are safe to put straight into a link.

60
00:02:37,117 --> 00:02:39,067
Compressed links are wonderful:

61
00:02:39,067 --> 00:02:41,459
nothing to store, nothing to expire,

62
00:02:41,459 --> 00:02:43,502
and the code travels with the link.

63
00:02:43,502 --> 00:02:45,546
But they still grow with the code.

64
00:02:45,546 --> 00:02:49,598
And a QR code's size grows with
the length of what it holds.

65
00:02:49,598 --> 00:02:53,313
A compressed link to a full
scene makes a dense code,

66
00:02:53,313 --> 00:02:58,398
with dots too small to scan comfortably
from a video in the corner of a screen.

67
00:02:58,398 --> 00:03:00,465
For this series, we wanted short,

68
00:03:00,465 --> 00:03:04,075
fixed-length links that
point to code of any size.

69
00:03:04,075 --> 00:03:06,750
That needs option two.

70
00:03:06,750 --> 00:03:09,606
Option two needs a name
for each piece of code.

71
00:03:09,606 --> 00:03:11,313
And here's the elegant answer:

72
00:03:11,313 --> 00:03:12,880
let the code name itself.

73
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:16,131
A cryptographic hash function is
a machine that takes any data,

74
00:03:16,131 --> 00:03:20,589
a word or a whole library, and
produces a fixed-size fingerprint.

75
00:03:20,589 --> 00:03:23,085
The one used here, called SHA five twelve,

76
00:03:23,085 --> 00:03:25,326
produces five hundred and twelve bits,

77
00:03:25,326 --> 00:03:27,288
shown here as a grid of squares.

78
00:03:27,288 --> 00:03:29,227
It has three properties that matter.

79
00:03:29,227 --> 00:03:31,061
First, it's deterministic:

80
00:03:31,061 --> 00:03:33,615
the same input always
gives the same fingerprint,

81
00:03:33,615 --> 00:03:35,380
on any computer, forever.

82
00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:37,203
Second, the avalanche effect.

83
00:03:37,203 --> 00:03:39,327
Change one letter, Manim to Manin,

84
00:03:39,327 --> 00:03:41,150
and about half of the bits flip:

85
00:03:41,150 --> 00:03:44,795
two hundred and sixty six of five
hundred and twelve in this case,

86
00:03:44,795 --> 00:03:47,013
scattered with no visible pattern.

87
00:03:47,013 --> 00:03:50,263
A small change to the code doesn't
give a similar fingerprint.

88
00:03:50,263 --> 00:03:52,167
It gives an unrelated one.

89
00:03:52,167 --> 00:03:55,720
Third, nobody knows any
practical way to work backwards,

90
00:03:55,720 --> 00:03:59,331
or to find two different inputs
with the same fingerprint.

91
00:03:59,331 --> 00:04:03,441
So in practice, a fingerprint stands
for exactly one piece of data.

92
00:04:03,441 --> 00:04:06,077
Scan this one and change either message.

93
00:04:06,077 --> 00:04:09,117
Every edit reshuffles the grid.

94
00:04:09,117 --> 00:04:12,670
HashBin, the storage service
ManimOnline saves to,

95
00:04:12,670 --> 00:04:14,260
names everything this way.

96
00:04:14,260 --> 00:04:16,768
Its addresses are called
content identifiers,

97
00:04:16,768 --> 00:04:19,601
or C I Ds, and they have two parts.

98
00:04:19,601 --> 00:04:23,293
The first eight characters encode
the content's length in bytes.

99
00:04:23,293 --> 00:04:24,999
The rest depends on the size.

100
00:04:24,999 --> 00:04:27,484
If the content is sixty
four bytes or less,

101
00:04:27,484 --> 00:04:29,585
the rest is simply the content itself,

102
00:04:29,585 --> 00:04:31,373
written in a link-safe alphabet.

103
00:04:31,373 --> 00:04:33,649
That's what our opening QR code was:

104
00:04:33,649 --> 00:04:35,901
an address that contains its own content,

105
00:04:35,901 --> 00:04:37,689
so nothing needs to be fetched at all.

106
00:04:37,689 --> 00:04:42,077
For anything longer, the rest is the
content's SHA five twelve fingerprint.

107
00:04:42,077 --> 00:04:46,339
To load it, the editor asks HashBin's
server for the content with that name.

108
00:04:46,339 --> 00:04:47,732
Now think about what this means.

109
00:04:47,732 --> 00:04:49,915
The name isn't chosen by anyone.

110
00:04:49,915 --> 00:04:51,946
It's calculated from the content.

111
00:04:51,946 --> 00:04:54,919
If someone changed a single
character of a stored scene,

112
00:04:54,919 --> 00:04:56,381
it would have a different fingerprint,

113
00:04:56,381 --> 00:04:57,739
and so a different name.

114
00:04:57,739 --> 00:05:01,083
The old address can only
ever mean the original bytes.

115
00:05:01,083 --> 00:05:02,813
And because the recipe is public,

116
00:05:02,813 --> 00:05:04,624
anyone can download the content,

117
00:05:04,624 --> 00:05:08,050
recompute its fingerprint,
and check that it matches.

118
00:05:08,050 --> 00:05:11,777
So every QR code in this series
encodes the ManimOnline address,

119
00:05:11,777 --> 00:05:14,029
a hash sign, and a C I D.

120
00:05:14,029 --> 00:05:16,072
That's about a hundred
and thirty characters,

121
00:05:16,072 --> 00:05:18,766
whether the scene behind it is
five lines long or a hundred.

122
00:05:18,766 --> 00:05:22,818
Short, fixed-length links make QR
codes with comfortably large dots.

123
00:05:22,818 --> 00:05:24,838
QR codes also carry error correction:

124
00:05:24,838 --> 00:05:26,556
a portion of each code is redundant,

125
00:05:26,556 --> 00:05:30,167
so it still scans if part of it is blurred
by video compression or hidden behind

126
00:05:30,167 --> 00:05:30,643
your thumb.

127
00:05:30,643 --> 00:05:33,301
And because the addresses
are computed from the code,

128
00:05:33,301 --> 00:05:36,680
we knew every QR code's address
before anything was uploaded.

129
00:05:36,680 --> 00:05:40,140
Saving the exact same bytes
always produces the same name.

130
00:05:40,140 --> 00:05:41,812
There is one honest caveat.

131
00:05:41,812 --> 00:05:44,610
A content address guarantees
what the content is,

132
00:05:44,610 --> 00:05:46,359
not that someone is still storing it.

133
00:05:46,359 --> 00:05:50,519
HashBin keeps content for a retention
period that's paid for in advance.

134
00:05:50,519 --> 00:05:53,305
If that lapses, the address
still means the same code,

135
00:05:53,305 --> 00:05:54,954
but nobody is serving it any more.

136
00:05:54,954 --> 00:05:56,893
Inline addresses, like the opening one,

137
00:05:56,893 --> 00:06:00,583
can never expire, because
there's nothing to serve.

138
00:06:00,583 --> 00:06:03,091
One last piece completes the picture.

139
00:06:03,091 --> 00:06:05,564
The editor itself is just files too,

140
00:06:05,564 --> 00:06:08,884
and the same care about exact
content applies to them.

141
00:06:08,884 --> 00:06:12,785
A service worker, a small script
that browsers let a site install,

142
00:06:12,785 --> 00:06:15,699
keeps a complete local copy of an exact,

143
00:06:15,699 --> 00:06:17,952
versioned list of the editor's files,

144
00:06:17,952 --> 00:06:19,902
including the Python runtime.

145
00:06:19,902 --> 00:06:24,593
Only when every file is safely stored
does the editor say Ready offline.

146
00:06:24,593 --> 00:06:26,102
When a new version is released,

147
00:06:26,102 --> 00:06:30,049
the new set is downloaded in full
before it replaces the old one,

148
00:06:30,049 --> 00:06:33,927
so you never end up running half
of one version and half of another.

149
00:06:33,927 --> 00:06:38,900
And unsaved code in an open tab
is preserved through the switch.

150
00:06:38,900 --> 00:06:41,315
Links can carry code in two ways.

151
00:06:41,315 --> 00:06:45,169
They can contain it, compressed by
noticing what's been said before.

152
00:06:45,169 --> 00:06:47,642
Or they can name it,
by its own fingerprint,

153
00:06:47,642 --> 00:06:50,824
so that the name can never
be pointed at anything else.

154
00:06:50,824 --> 00:06:52,646
And that brings us full circle.

155
00:06:52,646 --> 00:06:56,420
We started by noticing that an
animation is a stack of frames,

156
00:06:56,420 --> 00:06:59,670
and that a precise description
of change is a program.

157
00:06:59,670 --> 00:07:01,981
We gave that program nouns and verbs,

158
00:07:01,981 --> 00:07:04,349
curves and equations, things that change,

159
00:07:04,349 --> 00:07:05,719
graphs and cameras.

160
00:07:05,719 --> 00:07:07,298
Then we looked underneath:

161
00:07:07,298 --> 00:07:09,702
Python running inside a browser tab,

162
00:07:09,702 --> 00:07:12,778
an engine rebuilt and
measured until it matches,

163
00:07:12,778 --> 00:07:15,112
and links that carry the code you write.

164
00:07:15,112 --> 00:07:18,293
Every scene in this
series is one scan away,

165
00:07:18,293 --> 00:07:19,674
ready for you to change.

166
00:07:19,674 --> 00:07:22,878
The best way to understand
any of it is to break it,

167
00:07:22,878 --> 00:07:25,456
fix it, and make it your own.

168
00:07:25,456 --> 00:07:27,058
So go and make something.
